  I used the phrase "in game" to specify what you refer to as "in
character."  "Out of game" corresponds to your "out of character."  I
think my phrases fit English better, but you may feel differently.

  "In character" would seem better fitted for in-game actions
appropriate to the character's role/race/alignment/conducts.   Lawfuls
#dipping for Excalibur would be in character.  So would Wizards
casting spells, Knights jousting, Rogues backstabbing, etc.  

  "Out of character" would be actions inappropriate for
role/race/alignment/conducts.  Knights hitting fleeing enemies,
Samurai using poison, monks hitting with a wielded weapon, pacifists
killing monsters, etc are all out of character.  

  This division seems natural since the character doesn't really exist
outside of the game, but can be affected by out-of-game actions.



